WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.862
To identify correlation, look for an alias that was defined outside the subquery but used inside it.

00:00:06.862 --> 00:00:13.487
If the inner query needs p patient ID from the outer patient row, it is correlated.

00:00:13.487 --> 00:00:20.317
A quick diagnostic is to ask whether the inner query can run meaningfully by itself unchanged.

00:00:20.317 --> 00:00:23.927
If it needs an outer value, the answer is no.

00:00:23.927 --> 00:00:28.887
A correlated subquery refers to the current row of the outer query.

00:00:28.887 --> 00:00:34.292
Conceptually, the inner query is evaluated in relation to each outer row.

00:00:34.292 --> 00:00:39.757
That makes some business rules easy to express, but it can be expensive at scale.

00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:45.058
Compare a correlated formulation with a pre-aggregated join when performance matters.
